prior_art_librarian HIGH — the arm order, against the SYMBOL. save_page_sections_action.go matchLockedRow, read verbatim at HEAD 57336c127:
func matchLockedRow(lockedRows []*lockedPageRow, sectionName, sectionComponentID string) *lockedPageRow {
	if sectionComponentID != "" {
		for _, lr := range lockedRows {
			if !lr.consumed && lr.componentID != "" && lr.componentID == sectionComponentID {
				return lr
			}
		}
	}
	if sectionName == "" {
		return nil
	}
	for _, lr := range lockedRows {
		if !lr.consumed && lr.slot == sectionName {
			return lr
		}
	}
	norm := NormalizeComponentFunction(sectionName)
	if norm == "" {
		return nil
	}
	for _, lr := range lockedRows {
		if !lr.consumed && lr.slot != "" && NormalizeComponentFunction(lr.slot) == norm {
			return lr
		}
	}
	return nil
}


So the guard's arms are: (1) IDENTITY on component_id when the entry has one, (2) slot exact, (3) slot kebab-normalised (NormalizeComponentFunction, now datahelpers.NormalizeComponentFunction via delegate) — consume-once via lr.consumed. The merge (datahelpers.MergeLockedPageSlots) pairs: (1) slot exact, (2) slot kebab, (3) component function OR name — consume-once. The identity arm cannot be applied in the loader because the LIST is []string with no ids; identity is resolved LATER (plan_sections Path 0 loadPageSlotComponentIDs; save's enrichSectionsWithComponentIDs), and the guard applies its identity arm THEN on the entry's resolved component_id. Arm 3 of the merge is the loader-side stand-in for exactly that: a plan entry naming the component a locked row RENDERS will pair with that row by identity at save time, so the merge must count it as present — otherwise the merge inserts a second entry (the row's slot_name), and at save the plan entry consumes the locked row by identity while the merged entry finds its own row consumed, falls through slot/kebab (its own slot, consumed), and is INSERTed as a fresh copy beside the locked original: the bugs_open/189 shape. That is the case `already present by component function → no duplicate` in the table test, plus `consume-once with duplicate component` (two locked rows of one component vs a plan naming it once: pair one, insert the other, and at save identity-first pairs both rows in position order — tested reasoning in the lane NOTES). The design was arm-for-arm from the start; round 1's SUBMISSION had not shown the symbol. It is now also in the LOCK-008 register entry.

